## Experiment 10: Job Scheduling Using Branch and Bound

**Q1. What is Job Scheduling?**

Ans: Job Scheduling is the process of assigning jobs to available time slots while satisfying specified constraints and optimizing an objective.

**Q2. Which algorithm technique is used in this experiment?**

Ans: Branch and Bound.

**Q3. What is the objective of this Job Scheduling problem?**

Ans: To maximize the total profit obtained by completing selected jobs within their deadlines.

**Q4. What is a job deadline?**

Ans: The latest time by which a job must be completed to earn its profit.

**Q5. What is job profit?**

Ans: The reward obtained when a job is completed within its deadline.

**Q6. How much execution time does each job require in our implementation?**

Ans: One unit of time.

**Q7. What is Branch and Bound?**

Ans: An algorithm design technique that explores possible solutions and eliminates branches that cannot improve the best known solution.

**Q8. What is branching?**

Ans: Dividing a problem into smaller subproblems representing different choices.

**Q9. What is bounding?**

Ans: Estimating the best possible result obtainable from a partial solution.

**Q10. What is pruning?**

Ans: Eliminating branches that cannot produce a better or feasible solution.

**Q11. What is an upper bound?**

Ans: An estimate of the maximum possible objective value obtainable from a branch.

**Q12. What is a lower bound?**

Ans: A value that does not exceed the optimal objective value. In maximization, the best feasible profit found so far provides a lower bound.

**Q13. What is the upper bound formula used in our program?**

Ans: Upper Bound = Current Profit + Remaining Possible Profit.

**Q14. When is a branch eliminated?**

Ans: When its upper bound is less than or equal to the maximum profit already obtained.

**Q15. What are the two branches generated for each job?**

Ans: Including the job and excluding the job.

**Q16. What is a state space tree?**

Ans: A tree representing all possible states and decisions of a problem.

**Q17. What is a live node?**

Ans: A generated node that may still be expanded.

**Q18. What is a dead node?**

Ans: A node that will not be expanded further.

**Q19. What is an expansion node?**

Ans: A node currently being expanded to generate child nodes.

**Q20. What is a feasible schedule?**

Ans: A schedule in which selected jobs satisfy their deadlines and no two jobs occupy the same time slot.

**Q21. What is an optimal schedule?**

Ans: A feasible schedule that produces the maximum possible profit.

**Q22. Why are jobs sorted in descending order of profit?**

Ans: To consider high-profit jobs first and obtain a strong feasible solution early.

**Q23. Why is a job assigned to the latest available time slot?**

Ans: To preserve earlier time slots for jobs with tighter deadlines.

**Q24. Can two jobs occupy the same time slot?**

Ans: No, because our implementation assumes a single processor.

**Q25. Can a job be scheduled after its deadline?**

Ans: No, because it would not satisfy the scheduling constraints.

**Q26. What is the purpose of the Job structure?**

Ans: To store the job ID, deadline and profit.

**Q27. What is the purpose of the schedule array?**

Ans: To store the jobs assigned to time slots in the current partial solution.

**Q28. What is the purpose of the bestSchedule array?**

Ans: To store the best feasible schedule discovered so far.

**Q29. What is the purpose of maxProfit?**

Ans: To store the highest total profit obtained.

**Q30. What is the purpose of the remainingProfit array?**

Ans: To store the sum of profits of jobs that have not yet been considered.

**Q31. Why is remainingProfit calculated in advance?**

Ans: To calculate the upper bound in constant time.

**Q32. What is the worst-case candidate search space complexity?**

Ans: O(2^N), because each job can be included or excluded.

**Q33. What is the sorting time complexity?**

Ans: O(N log N).

**Q34. What is the recursion stack complexity?**

Ans: O(N).

**Q35. What is the total space complexity of our implementation?**

Ans: O(N), because the number of time slots is limited to the number of jobs.

**Q36. What is the difference between Branch and Bound and Backtracking?**

Ans: Backtracking mainly eliminates infeasible solutions, whereas Branch and Bound uses objective bounds to eliminate solutions that cannot improve the current best result.

**Q37. What is the difference between Greedy and Branch and Bound?**

Ans: Greedy selects locally optimal choices without reconsidering them, whereas Branch and Bound explores alternative choices using bounds.

**Q38. Can the classical unit-time Job Sequencing with Deadlines problem be solved using Greedy Method?**

Ans: Yes. Sorting jobs by descending profit and placing them in the latest available slot gives an optimal solution for the classical unit-time problem.

**Q39. Why is Branch and Bound used in this experiment?**

Ans: To demonstrate branching, bounding, pruning and systematic optimization as required by the experiment.

**Q40. What happens if all jobs have the same deadline of one?**

Ans: Only the job with the highest profit needs to be selected.

**Q41. What happens if all jobs can be completed within their deadlines?**

Ans: All positive-profit jobs can be selected to maximize the total profit.

**Q42. Can multiple optimal job schedules exist?**

Ans: Yes. Different schedules may produce the same maximum profit.

**Q43. What is FIFO Branch and Bound?**

Ans: A Branch and Bound technique that explores live nodes in first-in-first-out order using a queue.

**Q44. What is LIFO Branch and Bound?**

Ans: A Branch and Bound technique that explores live nodes in last-in-first-out order using a stack.

**Q45. What is Least Cost Branch and Bound?**

Ans: A technique that expands the live node with the most promising cost bound, commonly using a priority queue.

**Q46. Which search strategy is used in our implementation?**

Ans: Depth-first Branch and Bound using recursion.

**Q47. What is the maximum profit obtained in our first test case?**

Ans: 142.

**Q48. Which jobs are selected in our first test case?**

Ans: J3, J1 and J5.

**Q49. What are the applications of Job Scheduling?**

Ans: Task scheduling, production planning, manufacturing, resource allocation and project management.

**Q50. What is the main advantage of Branch and Bound?**

Ans: It eliminates unpromising branches while preserving the ability to find an optimal solution.

**Q51. What is the main disadvantage of Branch and Bound?**

Ans: Its worst-case search complexity can be exponential.

**Q52. Does Branch and Bound always guarantee polynomial-time execution?**

Ans: No. Its performance depends on the problem and the effectiveness of its bounding function.

**Q53. Why is Job Scheduling considered an optimization problem?**

Ans: Because it selects a feasible schedule that maximizes total profit.

**Q54. Can Branch and Bound be used for other optimization problems?**

Ans: Yes. It can be used for the Travelling Salesman Problem, Knapsack Problem, Assignment Problem and other combinatorial optimization problems.

**Q55. What is the termination condition of our recursive function?**

Ans: When all jobs have been considered or the current branch cannot improve the maximum profit.
